                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "5657323c-caed-3fd4-be26-0c037970abd5",
                        "entry_id": "8fe4700c-cb56-3648-8f42-bdab7d795798",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Conveyance, chiefly of goods, by boat."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f72514e5-0a24-4910-99d9-47506689b144",
                                "sense_id": "5657323c-caed-3fd4-be26-0c037970abd5",
                                "text": "At the verge of the town we had the misert of embarking on board another ferry-boat, the danger and destruction of horses; […] for they are oblig'd to leap out of, and into, deep water. […] This bad boatage, over a stream one mile broad is one of the causes of a new London road being open'd thro Llanwrst, which in a short time will eclipse the old Chester road."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "79196a78-2e0c-3d98-be13-b4dde071a0b6",
                        "entry_id": "8fe4700c-cb56-3648-8f42-bdab7d795798",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A charge for transporting goods or people by boat; (countable) an instance of this."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c9a8ff6b-01ee-4e5d-a94e-c8a523f9edba",
                                "sense_id": "79196a78-2e0c-3d98-be13-b4dde071a0b6",
                                "text": "Droict de Rivage. Shorage, or Boatage; the Cuſtome, or Toll for vvine, or other vvares, put vpon, or brought from, the vvater, by boats."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "02a7d2a5-81a1-4181-80c1-62765bb84467",
                                "sense_id": "79196a78-2e0c-3d98-be13-b4dde071a0b6",
                                "text": "Passengers by Steam on the Quarter Deck, and in the Best Cabin, Boatages to and from the Vessel included."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "dcb783a5-df6d-428f-8b29-2330f84df6b6",
                                "sense_id": "79196a78-2e0c-3d98-be13-b4dde071a0b6",
                                "text": "The Stockton organ was financed partly by the sale of pews in the newly erected organ gallery and partly by subscription. Around 80 subscribers (including Lord Darlington) subscribed £250 12s.; [Thomas] Griffin was paid £200 for building the organ and the rest of the money went towards boatage and porterage, painting and gilding, and the cost of hiring [John] Garth and the Durham singers to perform at the dedication."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "ba9774b0-f5b1-3f38-84e3-8585c9a80d5e",
                        "entry_id": "8fe4700c-cb56-3648-8f42-bdab7d795798",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The total capacity of a number of boats, especially of lifeboats on a ship."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "e015f173-beaa-4ce5-97be-24ece8e8361a",
                                "sense_id": "ba9774b0-f5b1-3f38-84e3-8585c9a80d5e",
                                "text": "The Titanic’s boatage and flotation equipment were also well above minimum requirements. She carried 3,560 life belts; 48 life buoys; 14 30-foot lifeboats; 2 emergency cutters; and 4 Englehardt collapsible rafts."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e0f25269-ef99-3065-810d-74651d50a1f5",
                        "entry_id": "8fe4700c-cb56-3648-8f42-bdab7d795798",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Boats collectively."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f0813c3f-8906-4628-811d-254ec7621405",
                                "sense_id": "e0f25269-ef99-3065-810d-74651d50a1f5",
                                "text": "For the Tovvn of Perith in Cumberland, he [William Strickland] cut a paſſage vvith great Art, Induſtry, and Expence, from the Tovvn into the river Petterill for the conveiance of Boatage into the Iriſh ſea."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
